---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import Grade from '../components/Grade.astro';
import State from '../components/State.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
---
<Base
  title="The DNA and genetics record"
  description="A claim-by-claim ledger of the DNA-related material in the public record: the 2014 sequencing, 23andMe kits, the Southern Trust informatics claim and the Zorro Ranch account, each marked as stated intent, documented activity or verified implementation."
  eyebrow="DNA and genetics continuum"
  lede="Several DNA-related items appear in the record. Read together without care, they can look like a programme. Read one at a time, they are a small sequencing job, some consumer test kits, a business description later alleged to be false, and a second-hand account of an idea."
  reviewed="2026-11-20"
  basis="Justice Department releases as reported; U.S. Virgin Islands filings"
>
  <h2 id="discipline">The three-state discipline</h2>
  <p>Each item on this page is placed in one of three states, and never promoted without a source.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><State s="intent" />: something was proposed or said.</li>
    <li><State s="activity" />: a record shows something was done.</li>
    <li><State s="verified" />: an official finding or independent result confirms the act and its outcome.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>No DNA-related item in this record reaches the third state.</p>

  <h2 id="ledger">Ledger</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap full">
    <table class="stack">
      <caption>DNA-related items in the public record</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Date</th><th>Evidence state</th><th>What the record supports</th><th>Grade</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Exome and fibroblast sequencing</th><td data-label="Date">June 2014</td><td data-label="Evidence state"><State s="activity" /></td><td data-label="What the record supports">A $2,000 initial charge on an invoice from Dr Joseph Thakuria, who says a cheque for that amount was paid.<Cite id="cnn-2026-thakuria" /></td><td data-label="Grade"><Grade g="A2" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Stem-cell, gene-editing and “Venus” proposals</th><td data-label="Date">June 2014</td><td data-label="Evidence state"><State s="intent" /></td><td data-label="What the record supports">Priced on the same invoice, to a total of $193,400. No record of payment or performance.<Cite id="cnn-2026-thakuria" /></td><td data-label="Grade"><Grade g="A2" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Cell lines from a tissue sample</th><td data-label="Date">2013</td><td data-label="Evidence state"><State s="activity" /></td><td data-label="What the record supports">Created within a research study in which he was enrolled as a subject; current whereabouts unknown.<Cite id="stat-2026-mystery" /></td><td data-label="Grade"><Grade g="A2" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th>23andMe kits for Woody Allen and Soon-Yi Previn</th><td data-label="Date">September 2016</td><td data-label="Evidence state"><State s="activity" /></td><td data-label="What the record supports">An email says kits arrived and would be delivered. Epstein replied “great.”<Cite id="nypost-2026-kits" /></td><td data-label="Grade"><Grade g="A2" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th>23andMe kits for Noam and Valeria Chomsky</th><td data-label="Date">April 2017</td><td data-label="Evidence state"><State s="activity" /></td><td data-label="What the record supports">A request from Karyna Shuliak; two kits bought on Amazon and shipped.<Cite id="nypost-2026-kits" /></td><td data-label="Grade"><Grade g="A2" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Bulk 23andMe order linked to Sultan Ahmed bin Sulayem</th><td data-label="Date">Summer 2017</td><td data-label="Evidence state"><State s="activity" /></td><td data-label="What the record supports">An order placed by an assistant; the company queried its size and cancelled it under its U.S.-use terms.<Cite id="sfgate-2026-kits" /></td><td data-label="Grade"><Grade g="A2" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Southern Trust “biomedical and financial informatics”</th><td data-label="Date">November 2012</td><td data-label="Evidence state"><State s="intent" /></td><td data-label="What the record supports">A description given to a tax-incentive commission. The government later alleged the company did no such work.<Cite id="usvi-complaint" /></td><td data-label="Grade"><Grade g="A1" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Zorro Ranch insemination idea</th><td data-label="Date">Early 2000s onward</td><td data-label="Evidence state"><State s="intent" /></td><td data-label="What the record supports">Reported as something he told people. The fullest account is second-hand. The Times reported no evidence it was acted on.<Cite id="nyt-2019-seed" /></td><td data-label="Grade"><Grade g="B2" /></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="sequencing">Limited personal sequencing</h2>
  <p>
    The only place the record shows genetic analysis of Epstein’s own material is the June 2014 invoice. Two items,
    $1,000 each, covered sequencing of his exome and of stored fibroblasts.<Cite id="cnn-2026-thakuria" /> The
    <a href="/harvard-medical-school/#invoice">Harvard Medical School page</a> sets out the full invoice and the
    statements of those involved.
  </p>

  <h2 id="kits">Commercial test kits</h2>
  <p>
    The Justice Department release contains emails in which Epstein’s staff buy and send 23andMe consumer kits as gifts.
    The best known is from April 2017, written by Karyna Shuliak:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>“Jeffrey would like to send 2 genetic kits from 23 and me to Noam and Valeria Chomsky, please. Could you please arrange that? I believe we have extra kits in the oval room closet, right?”</p>
    <footer>Email of April 2017, as quoted from the Justice Department release<Cite id="nypost-2026-kits" /></footer>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    The reply said two kits bought on Amazon had been shipped. An accountant, Bella Klein, asked that Epstein’s email be
    kept as backup for reimbursement.<Cite id="nypost-2026-kits" />
  </p>
  <p>
    In summer 2017 an assistant, Lesley Groff, placed a much larger order connected to Sultan Ahmed bin Sulayem and
    explained that he wanted kits for colleagues. 23andMe’s support staff questioned the volume and cancelled the order
    because its terms required use in the United States. Later correspondence says kits were nonetheless put on a flight
    to Dubai.<Cite id="sfgate-2026-kits" /> Accounts differ on the size of the order, from “over two dozen” to thirty
    kits costing a little over $6,000.
  </p>
  <Callout title="What the kit emails do and do not show" plain>
    <p>
      They show staff buying and posting an ordinary retail product. They do not describe a research protocol, the
      collection or pooling of results, or any link to the 2014 sequencing or to Southern Trust. Nothing in them shows
      whether a recipient used a kit, and receiving one implies nothing about the recipient.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="southern-trust">Southern Trust Company</h2>
  <p>
    In November 2012 Epstein and his lawyer appeared before the U.S. Virgin Islands Economic Development Commission and
    described Southern Trust Company as providing consulting in “biomedical and financial informatics.” In 2013 the
    company received a ten-year package of tax incentives.<Cite id="source-2022-settlement" />
  </p>
  <p>
    In its lawsuit against the estate, the territory’s Attorney General alleged that Southern Trust made fraudulent
    misrepresentations to obtain those benefits, and that it appeared to have had no clients and performed no visible
    informatics services.<Cite id="usvi-complaint" /> The case settled in November 2022 for more than $105 million.
    The government said the settlement would also return more than $80 million in tax benefits.<Cite id="usvi-2022-settlement" />
  </p>
  <p>
    These are allegations in a complaint that ended in a settlement. No court ruled on them. For present purposes the
    relevant point is narrower: there is no public output, paper, product or dataset showing that Southern Trust
    did genetic work of any kind.
  </p>

  <h2 id="zorro">The Zorro Ranch account</h2>
  <p>
    In July 2019 the New York Times reported that Epstein had told scientists and others of a wish to have women
    inseminated at his New Mexico ranch. The most specific version came from the author Jaron Lanier, who was relaying
    what a scientist had told him at a dinner. He did not hear it from Epstein. The Times reported that there was no
    evidence the idea came to anything.<Cite id="nyt-2019-seed" />
  </p>
  <p>We grade the newspaper’s reporting A2 and the relayed account B2. It is evidence of something said.</p>

  <h2 id="calibration">Outside calibration</h2>
  <p>
    How much weight should a stated plan carry? One reference point is the Repository for Germinal Choice, a California
    sperm bank that opened in 1980 with the stated aim of producing gifted children, and to which the Times account
    compared the ranch idea. The journalist David Plotz traced its history and found that it produced about 215
    children and did not produce what its founder promised.<Cite id="plotz-2005" /> Even an implemented plan of this
    kind did not do what its author said it would. An unimplemented one deserves less weight still.
  </p>

  <h2 id="law">The legal boundary: GINA</h2>
  <p>
    The Genetic Information Nondiscrimination Act of 2008 bars health insurers and employers with fifteen or more
    employees from using genetic information in coverage or employment decisions.<Cite id="gina-statute" /> It does not
    extend to life, disability or long-term-care insurance, and it does not create a general right of privacy in
    genetic data.<Cite id="nsgc-gina" /> It would bear on the material here only if a covered insurer or employer used
    someone’s results against them. No source reviewed alleges that.
  </p>

  <h2 id="assessment">Assessment</h2>
  <p>
    The items sit side by side and do not connect. The record shows no shared database, no research output and no
    operational programme. It shows one $2,000 sequencing job, proposals that went unfunded, retail kits sent as gifts,
    a business description that drew a fraud allegation, and a reported remark.
  </p>
</Base>
